Z||000000||loren||03-12-2001||11:03 AM||||i can,t get my 85 6cyl in time using a timing light,the timing mark on the damper ring is off location about 1 inch and i think i need a new vibration damper.<BR>is this an easy job or should i expect some problems.<BR>btw. i am the joker that has been asking so many questions about timing problems and i think that Will and oreif and others have finially got me on the right road<BR>thanks to all  Loren||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||03-12-2001||03:09 PM||||Sorry, Loren, but that was the last question you're allowed to ask.  What do you think we're here, for, anyway, to help you??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Replacing the damper will require some time, and usually any job is hard the first time around.  I would suggest you test for top-dead-center by probing the #1 spark plug hole with a straw or something while you rotate the crank with a wrench.  <B>After</B> you're sure the damper is indicating wrong, then I'd think about replacing it.    <P> ||||reg||12
Z||000002||loren||03-12-2001||05:23 PM||||<BR>i have definately diagnosed the problem and the damper ring is out of place at least 1 inch.<BR>i am running on " tune it by ear" and afraid it will damage the engine if i don't do something soon.<BR>i have a haynes manual and understand the text,but this being the first time i am a little nervous of the unknown.<BR>if you guys would help me see the light at the end of the tunnel (or at least pray for me) i sure would apperciate it.<BR>Loren ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cplensdorf||03-12-2001||05:47 PM||||So it was the balancer.  Like I said before, I went through the same process to find exactly what you did.  The balancer appears to be a simple task...but....it was kind of a PIA. You will need to get a puller that will fit between the damper and the cradle, it is tight.  If the damper goes back on right, you will be OK.  Mine, was really tight on the key..I had to 'force' it back on.  Removing the inner rear fender 'flap' makes the job a lot easier.  Just my thoughts.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cplensdorf||03-12-2001||05:51 PM||||Sorry, Loren, I posted my response to different post/ same issue.  Looks like you are not alone.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||03-12-2001||06:02 PM||||With the right tools replacing the damper isn't difficult, just tedious.<P>You'll need an impact wrench, a 1/2" drive socket (don't remember the size, I recommend a 6 point socket), and a 1'2" extension about 10" long.  You will also need a puller.  I got a puller set from Harbor Freight.  It has several plates and a variety of bolts with the center bolt and end fittings.  AutoZone should have something similar (maybe even in a loaner set).<P>Jack up the rear enough to get the r/r tire off the ground and put it on jack stands.<P>Remove the flexible inner fender curtain.  Do this by pulling out the center button of the plastic revets avout 3/8" or so and the rivets should pull out of the holes.  There are about 8 or 10 of them.<P>Carefully thread the curtain out from between the suspension parts  so you don't tear it any more than it already is.<P>Now you can see the damper.  There is a large bolt in the center and I believe 4 smaller bolts around it.<P>Loosen the alternator belt and the A/C belt and move them out of the way.<P>With the trans in Park (or in low gear if standard) use the impact wrench to bust the center bolt loose and remove it.  I have an electric imact wrench that I got from Harbor Freight for $69.00.  It developes 250 ft-lbs and has worked fine so far.<P>Remove the remaining 4 bolts and take the pulley off.<P>Hook up the puller and use the impact wrench to drive the damper off.<P>You might as well change out the timing cover seal while the damper is off.  It only costs around $5.00.<P>Wipe down the crankshaft with some motor oil.<P>Paint the timing mark on the new damper with white-out or something else that will be easy to see.<P>Apply a coat of motor oil to the interior and exterior of the shaft of the damper and slide it onto the crankshaft ensuring that the woodruf key is engaged in the slot.<P>Tap it in with a mallet and a block of wood until the center mounting bolt can grab at least 4 threads.  Then use the impact wrench to drive the damper onto the shaft.<P>I can't remember if the center bolt also holds the pulley on.  If so, remove the center bolt and put the pulley on with all five bolts.<P>Put the belts back on and tension them.  This is also a good time to install new belts.  The Belt Tensioner that JC Whitney and Harbor Freight sells is very handy.<P>Replace the curtain and the wheel and you are done.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||03-12-2001||06:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Carrolles:<BR><B>...Paint the timing mark on the new damper with white-out or something else that will be easy to see...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>...Thanks, Carrolles!  You knew if you didn't say something about painting the timing mark, I'd have to, didn't you?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||voyagerspe||03-12-2001||07:11 PM||||I just built a new motor and the Brand new <BR>balancer mark is off.  so I just painted a line once I confirmed TDC on the # 1 plug.<BR>no need to change it<BR>||||reg||1
